### Justifications of Goal Types

| Goal ID | Chosen Type | Reasoning |
|---------|-------------|------------|
| G1, G2, G3, G4, G5 | **Perform** | These goals describe concrete actions the robot must execute (e.g., moving, placing, handing over). The outcome depends solely on whether the actions are performed. |
| G4.1 | **Query** | It requires retrieving information from the inpatient record and presence of a companion; the goal is satisfied when the information is obtained. |
| G4.2, G5.3 | **Perform** | They decide between alternative execution paths (table vs. tray, door open vs. not). The robot must perform the chosen action. |
| All other sub‑goals | **Perform** | They involve operational steps such as indicating meals, tracking retrieval, alerting wrong meal, calling robot, signaling door availability – all are actions to be executed. |

---

This goal–task model captures the hierarchical decomposition of the multi‑robot food delivery mission, explicitly specifies runtime execution semantics, and clarifies the logical relations among goals and tasks.
